# DESCRIPTION

A native program is one built for `<arch>-unknown-vectraos`, the target ADR-0033 adds beside the POSIX one (posix(7)): ISO C23 against a C library of clause 7 alone, with no POSIX name in it, under the system's own calls.
The C library is llvm-libc, built from the toolchain's llvm-project on its baremetal layer (LLVM patch 0004), which makes no system call: it calls the system through hooks, which `libvx` supplies (M6 step 6e2b).
First-party system code does not use it; Swift's runtime, C and C++ programs from elsewhere, and their libraries do.

## The sysroot

`./build` makes the sysroot `out/<arch>/<mode>/vectraos/` when it finds the C library:

: `usr/include/`
  llvm-libc's headers, and the ABI's as `<vx/...>`.
: `usr/lib/crt1.o`
  vx-rt's entry, `_start`, and the native `vx_main`, C's `main` then `exit` (`lib/vx-rt/start.c`).
: `usr/lib/libvx.so`, `usr/lib/libvx.a`
  vx-rt and vx-ns with the C library's hooks (`lib/vx-rt/libvx.c`): the program starts as a first-party one does (start(2)), then `crt1.o`'s `vx_main` calls `main(argc, argv)` and gives its value to `exit`. A program links `libvx.so` by default, and is started by ld-vx(8), which loads it from `/lib/libvx.so` (M6 step 6f1b1, ADR-0047); `-static` links `libvx.a` instead. The C library and libc++ are archives either way, in the program; so `libvx.so` takes nothing from them, and has its own `mem*`, hidden.
: `usr/lib/libc.a`, `usr/lib/libm.a`
  llvm-libc's.
: `usr/lib/libclang_rt.builtins.a`
  compiler-rt's builtins.
: `usr/include/c++/v1/`, `usr/lib/libc++.a`, `usr/lib/libc++abi.a`
  libc++ and libc++abi (ADR-0033 §2a), from `swift-on-vectra`'s `build_cxx.sh` at the tree `cxx.sha256` records: RTTI on; threads through libc++'s external threading API, whose header (LLVM patch 0011) sits on the C library's `<threads.h>`, so `std::thread`, the mutexes, condition variables, `call_once`, guarded function-local statics and `thread_local` are vx-rt's threads and futexes; `steady_clock` on `timespec_get`'s `TIME_MONOTONIC` (patch 0010). iostreams in the C locale (LLVM patch 0005's locale support: `"C"` and `""` and nothing else), and `std::random_device` from the process's generator, seeded from the kernel's entropy (patch 0012). `std::filesystem` through a vectraos branch in libc++'s `src/filesystem` (patch 0013) over `libvx`'s file calls (`lib/vx-rt/libvx-fs.c`), no POSIX in between: paths UTF-8, symbolic links followed in the client, `fstream`s over the C library's streams. No exceptions until 6f2a, no wide characters. A machine without them builds the sysroot for C alone.
: `<arch>-unknown-vectraos-clang++.cfg`, `link-tail-c++.rsp`
  C++'s configuration file, which `clang++` reads in place of the C one (`-stdlib=libc++`, `-fno-exceptions`, the C file's other options, and no `-std`), and the link's tail with `-lc++ -lc++abi` first.
: `<arch>-unknown-vectraos.cfg`
  The configuration file Fedora's clang compiles with (ADR-0033 §3): `__vectraos__`, the sysroot and nothing of the host's, frame pointers, unwind tables, no PIC, no math `errno`. It names no language: a program chooses, and without `-std` it is the compiler's default, gnu17, as the Swift toolchain's patched clang has it. The system's own native programs are C23 (`-std=c23`); nothing makes a third party's be.
: `link-head.rsp`, `link-tail.rsp`
  `ld.lld`'s options before and after a program's objects: at `_start`, interpreter `/lib/ld-vx`, `-z now`, `-z relro`, both hash tables, `crt1.o` first; `libvx` (the shared one), `libc` and `libm` in a group, then the builtins. With `-static` before them, a static program.
: `llvm-libc`
  Which build of the C library it took.

Fedora's clang and the Swift toolchain's patched clang, which knows the target without a configuration file, must agree (ADR-0033 §3): where the Swift toolchain is there too, `./build` compiles a probe with both, in C and, with libc++ there, in C++, and stops, naming the difference, if their predefined macros (but for those an LLVM version brings), the code generation the configuration sets (frame pointers, unwind tables, relocation model, DWARF version with `-g`, `-std`) or their link lines differ.
A program defining `_POSIX_C_SOURCE`, `_XOPEN_SOURCE`, `_GNU_SOURCE` or the like is refused when it includes a header of the C library: it is ISO C's clause 7 alone, and a POSIX program is built for the POSIX personality (posix(7)); `./build` checks that it is refused.
First-party code never sees a hosted header: it is compiled with `-nostdlibinc`, and `./build check` runs clang-tidy and the analyzer over native programs with the sysroot's flags.

Until ADR-0034's first gate puts the toolchain in this tree, the C library comes from `swift-on-vectra`'s build (`toolchain/build_libc.sh`), found in `$VECTRA_SWIFT_ON_VECTRA` or `../../lang/swift-on-vectra`.
`./build` takes it only if its tree hash is the one `lib/vx-rt/sysroot/llvm-libc.ndb` records, so a new build is taken by recording it; with none there it builds without the sysroot, and an image that wants a native program stops.

## The hooks

: `FILE` streams
  llvm-libc's streams over a back end of the system's (LLVM patch 0007): `stdin` (fully buffered), `stdout` (line buffered) and `stderr` (unbuffered) are handles 0 to 2, which reach the program's standard pipes or the console through vx-rt, and `printf`, `puts` and `fprintf(stdout, ...)` share one stream; `fopen` opens a file of the process's namespace by vx-ns, `"r"` an existing one, `"w"` one made or emptied, `"a"` one written at its end however long it has grown, `"+"` both ways. `fseek` and `ftell` work on files, and refuse the standard streams (`ESPIPE`). At most 64 files are open at once.
: `exit`
  The value is the exit string, empty for 0 and the number in decimal otherwise, as the POSIX environment's: rc(1) shows `exit 7` as `name pid: 7`. `atexit`'s and `__cxa_atexit`'s handlers run first, last registered first, then `.fini_array`; `quick_exit` runs `at_quick_exit`'s instead.
: `errno`
  A thread-local word of `libvx`'s.
: `malloc` and the rest
  The process heap (heap(2)); `aligned_alloc` takes alignments up to 64 KiB.
: `getenv`
  `/env/NAME` (envd(4)), as 9front's APE reads it: a list's words joined by spaces. A process with no `/env` has the spawn message's `env=` records. A value stays good until the next `getenv` of the same name.
: `remove`, `rename`
  The namespace's files; `rename` within one server, and within one directory on a server with neither 9P2000.L nor the POSIX extension.
: Futexes
  The C library's locks (LLVM patch 0006) wait and wake through the kernel's futexes, `libvx`'s `vx_futex_wait` and `vx_futex_wake`.
: `<threads.h>`
  C11's threads (LLVM patch 0008) are vx-rt's (thread(2)), each with its own stack, of at least 256 KiB, and its own copy of the program's thread-local storage, the C library's included. `thrd_exit` ends a thread from anywhere below its function, and its value reaches `thrd_join`; a detached thread is freed by the next `thrd_create` or `thrd_join` after it ends. `mtx_recursive` mutexes keep their owner. `tss_create`'s destructors run as each thread ends. `mtx_timedlock` and `cnd_timedwait` take `TIME_UTC` deadlines, turned into the monotonic clock's (LLVM patch 0009); `thrd_sleep` sleeps the whole duration, and `thrd_yield`, the kernel having no yield, is the shortest sleep. The heap under `malloc` is safe between threads.
: `setlocale`
  One locale, `"C"`: by that name, or `""`, the environment's; a query gives `"C"`, and any other name fails.
: `timespec_get`, `clock`
  `TIME_UTC` is the wall clock; `clock` is the CPU time the whole process has had (ADR-0041).

## Swift

Swift programs build with `swift-on-vectra`'s toolchain and the SDK its `toolchain/build_stdlib.sh` makes from this sysroot, `toolchain/out/sdk/<triple>`, with Swift's runtime over the same C library and hooks (M6 step 6e3, ADR-0034). They link as C programs do, dynamically against `libvx.so` (ld-vx(8)), with Swift's runtime, libc++ and the C library linked in statically (M6 step 6f1b3); `-static-executable` links a static one. Their platform module is `VectraOSLibc`, the C library's clause 7 headers (no `complex.h`), with `errno` and the type-generic math; `CommandLine.arguments` is vx-rt's argv, through `__swift_vectraos_argv`. Swift's Concurrency runs on VectraOS's own executors (Swift patch 0012): a worker thread per CPU the program may use (`__swift_vectraos_cpu_count`), and the main thread for the main actor once an async `main` hands it over; `Synchronization.Mutex` waits on the kernel's futexes. FoundationEssentials (`swift-on-vectra`'s `build_foundation.sh`, without ICU) reaches the system through the same hooks as the C and C++ libraries, under its own names, never POSIX's: files and directories as `std::filesystem` has them, files opened exclusively for atomic writes, and the process's id, user, host name (`/sys/name`), environment (`/env`, or the spawn message's `env=`) and the errno a status reads as (`__swift_vectraos_*`). Its time zone is GMT alone until VectraOS has a time zone database. Until ADR-0034's first gate brings the toolchain here, a scenario runs a Swift program built in that tree: `./build` takes the programs its `EXTERNAL_PROGRAMS` names from `tests/out/<triple>/` there.

# EXAMPLES

Compiling and linking a program by hand, as `./build` does:

```text
S=out/x86_64/debug/vectraos T=x86_64-unknown-vectraos
clang --config-system-dir=$S --target=$T -c hello.c
ld.lld --sysroot=$S @$S/link-head.rsp hello.o @$S/link-tail.rsp -o hello
clang++ --config-system-dir=$S --target=$T -std=c++23 -c app.cpp
ld.lld --sysroot=$S @$S/link-head.rsp app.o @$S/link-tail-c++.rsp -o app
```

